clueweb09-en0011-39-26948.0 Landforms and Other Geologic Features :: Basins to Dunes Continental Margin They are the drowned edges of continents. Covered by the oceans, continental margins are part of the same crust (thin, solid outermost layer of Earth) that forms the continents. They are covered in sediments that have been eroded from dry land. (Erosion is the gradual wearing away of Earth surfaces through the action of wind and water.) Lying between the deep ocean basins and the above-water land areas, continental margins account for 11 percent of Earth's surface. The shape of the land The continental margin is the submerged outer
clueweb09-enwp00-46-14291.0 Plate tectonics From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia (Redirected from Continental plates) Jump to: navigation, search [] [] The tectonic plates of the world were mapped in the second half of the 20th century. Plate tectonics (from Greek ýýýýýýýýýýýý, tektýýn "builder" or "mason") describes the large scale motions of Earth's lithosphere. The theory encompasses the older concepts of continental drift, developed during the first half of the 20th century, and seafloor spreading, understood during the 1960s. The outermost part of the Earth's interior is made up of two layers: above is the lithosphere, comprising the crust and the rigid uppermost part
